I Built a Cozy 60 FPS AI Agent Bakery Game (Built with Gemma 4 & Antigravity) to Teach RAG, Guardrails & Multi-Agent Orchestration

## What I Built

Who said learning **AI System Design** has to be all dark cyberpunk terminal screens and exploding servers?

I built and open-sourced **Prompt Paws: The Cozy AI Agent Cafe & Bakery** (architected with **Google Gemma 4 & Antigravity**) — an interactive **60 FPS AI Agent Architecture Training & Simulation Platform** disguised as a cozy pastel pastry bakery run by **Robot-Cat Chefs**! 🐱‍🍳🍰

**Who would use it?**
- **Engineering Teams, Bootcamps & Developer Relations Programs** training developers on production LLM architecture (where a single uncached system prompt or missing input guardrail can cost thousands of dollars in wasted tokens or security incidents).
- Instead of static slide decks, **Prompt Paws** gives teams an interactive, visual sandbox where every pastry station maps **1-to-1** to a real production AI pattern:

| Cozy Bakery Station | Critter Order Handled | Production AI Engineering Layer |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **🥐 Flash Pastry Oven** | Prompt Croissant | **Fast Low-Latency Model (`gemini-2.5-flash` / Gemma 4)** |
| **🍪 Context Cache Jar** | Repeat Owl Menu Query | **Prefix & Context Caching (95% Token Savings)** |
| **🛡️ Guardrail Flour Sieve** | Spicy Injection Jalapeño | **Input Classifier & Prompt-Injection Sanitizer** |
| **🍓 Vector Berry Patch** | RAG Berry Tart | **Vector Search & Fact Grounding (Anti-Hallucination)** |
| **🧋 Tool-Call Boba Mixer** | Code-Exec Boba Tea | **Deterministic Python Sandbox & Function Calling** |
| **🍮 Deep-Think Soufflé Oven** | Reasoning Soufflé | **Extended Thinking / System-2 Deliberation** |
| **🐱 Subagent Sous-Chef Bell** | Parallel Banquet Aura | **Multi-Agent Parallel Delegation (+45% Speed Aura)** |

![Prompt Paws: Cozy AI Agent Cafe Banner](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/RobinaMirbahar/cozy-ai-agent-cafe-game/main/assets/cozy_ai_cafe_banner.jpg)

---

## 🎮 Live Playable Demo & How to Play in 30 Seconds!

- **🕹️ Play Live in Your Browser (60 FPS HTML5 Canvas):** 👉 **[https://robinamirbahar.github.io/cozy-ai-agent-cafe-game/](https://robinamirbahar.github.io/cozy-ai-agent-cafe-game/)**
- **🔒 100% Client-Side Simulation ($0.00 API Cost & Zero Keys Required):** The entire game runs locally inside your browser using a deterministic HTML5 Canvas simulation engine—**no API keys, no sign-ups, and zero paid LLM tokens** are ever used when you or your team play!

![How to Play Prompt Paws in 3 Easy Steps](https://files.catbox.moe/i9wg40.jpg)

### 🐾 What Is the Core Gameplay Loop?
Think of **Prompt Paws** as a **cozy Tower-Defense & Kitchen-Management game for AI Agent Architecture**:
- **Customer Pastry Orders (`🥐`, `🍪`, `🌶️`, `🍓`, `🧋`, `🍮`) = Incoming User Prompts** moving along the bakery conveyor belt toward the **Pickup Window (`🛎️`)**.
- **Robot-Cat Kitchen Stations = Real-World AI Engineering Components** (Fast LLMs, Context Caches, Input Guardrails, RAG Vector DBs, Deterministic Tool Callers, System-2 Reasoning Models, and Multi-Agent Sous-Chefs) that intercept and process prompts before they reach the customer!
- **Win / Lose Condition:** Every order baked by the right AI Chef Station earns **🪙 Star Coins**! If an unbaked order, an ungrounded **👻 Hallucination Puff**, or a sneaky **🌶️ Prompt Injection** slips all the way to the Pickup Window, you lose a **💖 Cafe Reputation Heart**!

### 🕹️ How to Play in 3 Easy Steps:
1. **STEP 1 — Pick an AI Chef Station (Left Menu):** Click a station card on the left sidebar (start with **🥐 Flash Pastry Oven** for `🪙 60`). Check the **Order Forecast chips** under the level story to see which prompt types are coming!
2. **STEP 2 — Click Any `[➕ Slot]` Tile on the Map:** Click one of the 8 pastel counter pads (`Slot 1`–`Slot 8`) beside the conveyor belt to place your Robot-Cat Chef. (Tip: Click a placed chef anytime to **⬆️ Upgrade** its baking speed or **🧹 Refund** it!)
3. **STEP 3 — Click `▶️ Open Cafe` (or `✨ Auto-Chef Blueprint`):**
   - Click **`▶️ Open Cafe`** (or press `Spacebar`) to start the shift and watch your Robot-Cat Chefs bake!
   - **Want to watch an instant demo first?** Click the purple **`✨ Auto-Chef Blueprint`** button in the top bar on any of the **7 Story Shifts** and the game will automatically build the optimal Guardrail, Cache, RAG, and Subagent pipeline and run it for you!

---

## 💻 Open-Source Code

{% github RobinaMirbahar/cozy-ai-agent-cafe-game %}

---

## 🛠️ How I Built It

I built **Prompt Paws** using an open-source, zero-dependency HTML5 Canvas 60 FPS simulation engine (`game.js`), a 7-shift progressive AI engineering curriculum (`levels.js`), and a procedural WebAudio synthesizer (`audio.js`), orchestrated via **Antigravity** and open agentic workflows.

### 1. Order Routing & Guardrail Sanitization (`game.js`)
Each station inspects passing orders on the winding bakery conveyor ribbon and only picks up tasks that match its architectural role. Notice how **Spicy Prompt-Injection Jalapeños** cannot be safely baked by normal ovens until a **Guardrail Flour Sieve** sanitizes them into safe croissants:

```javascript
function canStationProcessOrder(stationType, ord) {
  // 1. Only the Guardrail Flour Sieve can catch and sanitize Spicy Prompt Injections!
  if (stationType === 'guardrail_sieve') {
    return ord.kind === 'jalapeno';
  }
  // Unfiltered prompt injections bypass normal ovens and damage Cafe Reputation Hearts!
  if (ord.kind === 'jalapeno') {
    return false;
  }

  // 2. Context Cache Jar serves repeated menu queries at 3x speed + bonus Star Coins
  if (stationType === 'cookie_cache') {
    return ord.kind === 'cached' || ord.kind === 'croissant';
  }

  // 3. Vector Berry Patch grounds Berry Tarts so they don't become Hallucination Puffs
  if (stationType === 'vector_garden') {
    return ord.kind === 'tart';
  }

  // 4. Tool-Call Boba Mixer runs deterministic calculations & API recipes
  if (stationType === 'tool_blender') {
    return ord.kind === 'boba';
  }

  // 5. Deep-Think Souffle Oven handles multi-step System-2 reasoning
  if (stationType === 'reasoning_oven') {
    return ord.kind === 'souffle' || ord.kind === 'croissant';
  }

  // 6. Flash Pastry Oven handles high-throughput everyday Prompt Croissants
  if (stationType === 'flash_oven') {
    return ord.kind === 'croissant' || ord.kind === 'cached';
  }

  // 7. Subagent Sous-Chef Bell orchestrates parallel helpers
  if (stationType === 'subagent_bell') {
    return ord.kind !== 'jalapeno';
  }
  return false;
}
```

### 2. Real-Time AI Architecture Linter ("Chef Whiskers" 🐱‍🍳)
Before you open the cafe doors, **Chef Whiskers (Staff AI Pastry Architect)** performs live static analysis on your placed stations and warns you about architectural vulnerabilities:

```javascript
function evaluateKitchenArchitecture() {
  const lvl = window.CAFE_LEVELS[currentLevelIdx];
  const weights = lvl.orderWeights;
  const typesPlaced = Object.values(placedStations).map((s) => s.type);
  const alerts = [];

  if (weights.jalapeno && !typesPlaced.includes('guardrail_sieve')) {
    alerts.push({
      kind: 'warn',
      text: 'Prompt Injection Risk! Place a Guardrail Flour Sieve near Slot 1 or 2 to neutralize spicy raccoon orders!'
    });
  }
  if (weights.tart && !typesPlaced.includes('vector_garden')) {
    alerts.push({
      kind: 'warn',
      text: 'Hallucination Warning! Berry Tarts need a Vector Berry Patch (RAG) so chefs do not guess facts!'
    });
  }
  if (weights.boba && !typesPlaced.includes('tool_blender')) {
    alerts.push({
      kind: 'warn',
      text: 'Math/API Alert! Add a Tool-Call Boba Mixer to run deterministic calculations accurately.'
    });
  }
  return alerts;
}
```

---

## 🌐 Why Open-Source Visual Sandboxes Matter

Open-source AI models, open agent harnesses, and inspectable system design sandboxes ensure that **every developer—not just giant enterprises—can master safe, cost-efficient AI engineering**. By making the entire simulation engine, curriculum (`levels.js`), and architecture blueprints open-source with zero proprietary build steps and **$0.00 API token cost**, any educator or developer community can fork **Prompt Paws**, add new open-weight model stations (like local Gemma 4 or Ollama nodes), and teach agentic reliability visually!

Local image generation with llamadart for the Llama Village storybook

*Beyond chat with llamadart · Part 4*

Dash missed it. Pip’s scarf is by the pond, and all he has is the journal entry. Could a local image model draw the moment for the village storybook?

![Dash and Mo with the storybook](https://dev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com/uploads/articles/0ixa6396m1ripr4zvpq7.png)

*Concept cover made with image generation. The local model outputs below have a different, much simpler style.*

[Llama Village](https://dev.to/gde/llama-village-a-virtual-world-powered-by-local-ai-with-llamadart-3lmi) already retells a run with its journal and captured pictures. For this standalone experiment, I used llamadart’s `ImageGenerationEngine` to imagine a scene from a sample event. Two local models, three prompts, one thing to look for: **can you find the scarf?**

## Give the picture one job

Our sample event is small: **a red scarf caught in reeds beside the village pond.** We build the prompt in Dart. No language model is needed to rewrite it.

```dart
const memory = (
  subject: 'a red wool scarf caught in green reeds',
  place: 'beside a small village pond',
);
final prompt = '${memory.subject}, ${memory.place}, '
    'rainy evening, gentle storybook illustration, no lettering';
```

These are example journal fields, not an existing Llama Village API. Keeping facts separate from the art direction lets the game decide what the picture is about.

![Journal to local image generation, compact diagram](https://dev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com/uploads/articles/of0dx283hwgdzt1sclbk.png)

*Concept diagram: Dart builds the prompt; `ImageGenerationEngine` produces the image and reports progress separately. The illustrated book here is explanatory artwork.*

The engine runs stable-diffusion.cpp locally. For this first attempt I chose SDXS: one model file, about 683 MB, and a recipe that uses one generation step.

## A lovely pond. Where is the scarf?

![Rainy evening generated locally with SDXS](https://dev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com/uploads/articles/oo76ap5l5z2fvt1dop7x.png)

*Actual output: SDXS Q8_0, 512 × 512, one step, guidance 1, seed 42.*

There are reeds, water, houses, and rain. I like the atmosphere. But I cannot identify the scarf, which is the reason this scene belongs in the book.

Would a brighter scene make it easier to see? I changed only **“rainy evening” → “clear morning”**, keeping the model, seed, dimensions, and other settings:

![Clear morning generated locally with SDXS](https://dev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com/uploads/articles/c2pknbzj0rrmu2k11v4q.png)

*The sky brightens and the rain disappears. The scarf is still missing.*

A fixed seed helps us compare prompts on this setup. It does not lock the composition or guarantee identical pixels across runtime versions and devices.

## Put the important thing in the foreground

The weather changed; the missing object did not return. For the third image, I asked the model to put the scarf right in front of us:

> Close-up of one red knitted wool scarf tangled in green reeds, the scarf fills the foreground, pond water behind it, rainy evening, gentle storybook illustration, no lettering

![Close-up generated locally with SDXS](https://dev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com/uploads/articles/z152m9zqwoyie9y81qzj.png)

Now there is recognizable red knitted fabric. There is also extra green fabric, and the rain is not obvious. The prompt helped; it did not make the picture an exact record.

That is the storybook’s boundary: **the journal says what happened; the illustration imagines how it looked.** If the game needs exact character faces, positions, or evidence of an event, keep the captured screenshot.

## Can SD-Turbo find the scarf?

Perhaps a larger model would follow the scene more closely. I tried the same three prompts with **SD-Turbo Q8_0 + TAESD**: 512 × 512, one step, guidance 1, seed 42. TAESD is the small decoder in the [documented recipe](https://llamadart.leehack.com/docs/guides/image-generation), so this compares the complete recipes.

![SD-Turbo plus TAESD rainy-evening](https://dev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com/uploads/articles/cf4o9xloxlno02aseut7.png)

*SD-Turbo, rainy evening. More distinct foreground reeds, but still no clearly recognizable scarf. The rain is less obvious than in the SDXS image.*

![SD-Turbo plus TAESD clear-morning](https://dev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com/uploads/articles/3h18hqmd7skzn502gw1j.png)

*SD-Turbo, clear morning. Red shapes appear among the reeds, but they do not read clearly as one wool scarf. The weather change is subtle.*

![SD-Turbo plus TAESD close-up](https://dev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com/uploads/articles/aajao0m7mfn6o4bqkzpi.png)

*SD-Turbo, close-up. Recognizable red knitted pieces appear at both edges. It misses the request for one scarf filling the foreground.*

For this storybook scene, I prefer SDXS’s softer style. Neither model reliably follows every detail. This is a visual judgment about three prompts at one seed, not a general quality ranking.

| Recipe | Model files | Warm generation median | Peak process RSS |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| SDXS Q8_0 | 0.68 GB | 0.39 s | 1.51 GiB |
| SD-Turbo Q8_0 + TAESD | 2.03 GB | 0.58 s | 2.92 GiB |

Almost twice the process memory and three times the model storage still did not give us a clear picture of the event. I would keep SDXS for this prototype. That choice is specific to these scenes and settings; more SD-Turbo steps or its full decoder could change the result.

### Measurement details

M4 Max, 64 GB, Metal, Dart 3.13.5, llamadart 0.11.1. Each recipe ran alone in a fresh process with cached files. After one warm-up pass over all three prompts, I measured three more passes: nine generations per recipe.

- **Generation:** 0.383–0.387 s for SDXS; 0.545–0.622 s for SD-Turbo. Timing ends at `task.result`, before PNG encoding or disk writes. Downloads and model loading are excluded.

- **Memory:** process RSS sampled every 20 ms across setup, loading, generation, and saving. This does not measure total device or GPU memory.

- **Conditions:** renderer off, on a development machine rather than an isolated benchmark.

The comparison uses TAESD, not SD-Turbo’s full VAE. Four-step generation and the full decoder were not tested. Both model revisions and the decoder revision are pinned in the runnable program.

## What the Dart code does

To try the scarf scene yourself, the program follows four calls:

```dart
// 1. Check this build and device before downloading weights.
final runtime = await ImageGenerationEngine.checkRuntime();

// 2. Load a pinned ModelSource; the download manager caches it.
final engine = await ImageGenerationEngine.load(model);

// 3. Start a task, then listen for progress while it runs.
final task = await engine.generate(request);

// 4. Await completion and save the resulting pixels as PNG.
final result = await task.result;
await File('memory.png').writeAsBytes(result.images.first.toPng());
```

*This is the API outline. The complete program defines `model` and `request` inline, handles an unsupported runtime, and disposes the engine.*

`task.events` reports phases such as prompt encoding, sampling, and decoding. It does not stream finished pictures. Await `task.result` for the image or a failure. The program cancels its event subscription in `finally` and disposes the engine after all three pictures.

For SDXS, explicitly use `steps: 1` and `guidanceScale: 1`. Those settings belong to this distilled model; copying them to an arbitrary image model is not a useful comparison.

## Try the same three pictures

I ran the complete program on an M4 Max with Dart 3.13.5 and **llamadart 0.11.1**. `dart analyze` passed and both modes produced all three PNGs. Allow about 683 MB for SDXS, or 2.03 GB for SD-Turbo plus TAESD, plus the package and opt-in native runtime. The first run downloads the files; cached runs generate locally.

Create an empty directory, save this as `pubspec.yaml`, then run `dart pub get`:

```yaml
name: llama_storybook
publish_to: none
environment:
  sdk: ^3.10.7
dependencies:
  llamadart: 0.11.1
hooks:
  user_defines:
    llamadart:
      llamadart_native_runtimes: [stable_diffusion]
```

Image generation is a Preview API. This example is native Dart on macOS; Web has no image runtime. For Flutter Apple apps, follow the [image-generation setup guide](https://llamadart.leehack.com/docs/guides/image-generation) for the companion package and platform requirements.

<details>
<summary>Complete runnable program</summary>


Create a `bin` directory and save this as `bin/illustrate.dart`:

```dart
import 'dart:convert';
import 'dart:io';

import 'package:llamadart/llamadart.dart';

Future<void> main(List<String> args) async {
  if (args.any((arg) => arg != '--turbo')) {
    stderr.writeln('Usage: dart run bin/illustrate.dart [--turbo]');
    exitCode = 64;
    return;
  }
  final turbo = args.contains('--turbo');
  final runtime = await ImageGenerationEngine.checkRuntime();
  if (!runtime.isSupported) {
    stderr.writeln(runtime.unsupportedReason);
    exitCode = 1;
    return;
  }
  final engine = await ImageGenerationEngine.load(
    turbo
        ? ImageGenerationModel(
            ModelSource.huggingFace(
              repoId: 'Green-Sky/SD-Turbo-GGUF',
              revision: '19a31586d02d64a73b4419bc193b3ecfaf38e1f0',
              filePath: 'sd_turbo-f16-q8_0.gguf',
            ),
            components: [
              ImageModelComponent.auto(
                ModelSource.huggingFace(
                  repoId: 'madebyollin/taesd',
                  revision: '614f76814bbe30edbe2e627ace1c2234c81a2c0e',
                  filePath: 'diffusion_pytorch_model.safetensors',
                ),
              ),
            ],
          )
        : ImageGenerationModel(
            ModelSource.huggingFace(
              repoId: 'concedo/sdxs-512-tinySDdistilled-GGUF',
              revision: '3144d898d61492f8382ffcabec055733fc5b2a0e',
              filePath: 'sdxs-512-tinySDdistilled_Q8_0.gguf',
            ),
          ),
  );
  try {
    // A sample journal event. The game supplies these facts.
    const memory = (
      subject: 'a red wool scarf caught in green reeds',
      place: 'beside a small village pond',
    );
    for (final setting in ['rainy evening', 'clear morning', 'close up']) {
      final prompt = setting == 'close up'
          ? 'Close-up of one red knitted wool scarf tangled in green reeds, '
                'the scarf fills the foreground, pond water behind it, '
                'rainy evening, gentle storybook illustration, no lettering'
          : '${memory.subject}, ${memory.place}, '
                '$setting, gentle storybook illustration, no lettering';
      final watch = Stopwatch()..start();
      final task = await engine.generate(
        ImageGenerationRequest(
          prompt: prompt,
          width: 512,
          height: 512,
          steps: 1,
          guidanceScale: 1,
          seed: 42,
        ),
      );
      final progress = task.events.listen((event) {
        if (event case ImageGenerationProgressEvent(
          :final phase,
          :final step,
          :final steps,
        )) {
          stdout.writeln('$phase $step/$steps');
        }
      });
      try {
        final result = await task.result;
        final generationMs = watch.elapsedMilliseconds;
        final prefix = turbo ? 'turbo-' : '';
        final name = '$prefix${setting.replaceAll(' ', '-')}';
        final image = result.images.first;
        await File('$name.png').writeAsBytes(image.toPng());
        await File('$name.json').writeAsString(
          const JsonEncoder.withIndent('  ').convert({
            'prompt': prompt,
            'seed': result.seed,
            'width': image.width,
            'height': image.height,
            'steps': 1,
            'guidanceScale': 1,
            'generationMs': generationMs,
          }),
        );
        stdout.writeln('Saved $name.png in $generationMs ms');
      } finally {
        await progress.cancel();
      }
    }
  } finally {
    await engine.dispose();
  }
}
```

Run it from the project directory:

```sh
# SDXS
dart run bin/illustrate.dart

# SD-Turbo with TAESD
dart run bin/illustrate.dart --turbo
```

The default run writes `rainy-evening.png`, `clear-morning.png`, and `close-up.png`, each with a JSON file recording the prompt, seed, settings, and generation time. Turbo filenames start with `turbo-`, so you can compare both sets.

For another scene, replace the scarf with a blue umbrella in **both** `memory.subject` and the separate close-up prompt. Does moving the umbrella into the foreground help?

</details>

## Back to Dash’s storybook

The next step would be adding this to the game. When Dash opens the storybook, show the journal immediately. After dialogue and speech work finishes, generate the illustration, use progress events for the loading indicator, and replace the placeholder when the PNG is ready. If generation fails, keep the journal and any captured picture available.

Before putting this into the game, I would repeat the measurements with the renderer running and the other models loaded. That is the memory and latency budget the player will experience.

Dash could get an illustrated version of the moment he missed. But I would keep the journal beside the picture. A lovely pond is enough to set the mood; it is not enough to tell us what happened to Pip’s scarf.

Deep Dive: Autonomous Code Auditing, 40% Python Speedups & Stateful 1M-Token Agents with Gemini 4 Argon

![Robina's DeepSWE & Agentic Coding Guide with Gemini 4 Argon](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/RobinaMirbahar/gemini-4-argon-cookbook/main/assets/robina_argon_deepswe_infographic.jpg)

In my [previous post](https://dev.to/gde/building-a-live-3d-1m-token-engineering-cookbook-with-google-deepminds-gemini-4-argon-h1k), we explored how Google DeepMind's **Gemini 4 Argon (`gemini-4-argon`)** powers interactive 3D WebGL simulations and 3D ray-tracing engines.

In this deep dive, we will focus on **Software Engineering, Autonomous Security Auditing, and Stateful Multi-Turn Agentic Architectures** -- the capabilities behind Gemini 4 Argon's **77.9% SOTA score on DeepSWE v1.1**, its **40% session-prep speedup** on `google/adk-python`, and its **0.7% attack success rate on the Gray Swan Indirect Prompt Injection (IPI)** benchmark.

All production code in this article is open-source in **[github.com/RobinaMirbahar/gemini-4-argon-cookbook](https://github.com/RobinaMirbahar/gemini-4-argon-cookbook)**.

---

## Architecture Overview: Why Gemini 4 Argon Changes Agentic Software Engineering

Traditional LLM coding assistants struggle with three fundamental bottlenecks on real-world production repositories:
1. **Output Truncation on Whole-Module Refactors:** Standard 16K--64K output caps force models to emit partial diffs (`// ... existing code ...`), breaking AST validity on large files. Gemini 4 Argon unifies **1,000,000 input tokens and 1,000,000 output tokens** (a **15.6x output jump**) via the **Long-Decode Continuation Protocol**.
2. **Reasoning Amnesia Across Tool Calls:** When an agent executes 10+ sequential static-analysis or test-runner tools, traditional chat APIs lose the model's internal chain-of-thought between turns. The **Gemini Interactions API v2 (`client.interactions.create`)** introduces first-class `thought` outputs with cryptographic **`thought_signature`** verification and stateful **`previous_interaction_id`** chaining.
3. **Context Cost on Multi-Turn Loops:** Re-reading a 500K-token repository across 20 agent turns used to be prohibitively expensive. Gemini 4 Argon offers a **95% discount on cached input tokens (\$0.10 per 1M tokens vs \$2.00 per 1M standard input)**.

---

## Deep Dive 1: Building an Autonomous Security & 40%-Class Performance Code Auditor

During internal testing on Google's Agent Development Kit (`google/adk-python`), Gemini 4 Argon identified redundant per-turn session serialization overhead and refactored the hot path for a **40% end-to-end speedup**. On security benchmarks, it chains subtle multi-function flaws rather than just pattern-matching single lines.

Below is the complete architecture from [`examples/03_security_code_auditor.py`](https://github.com/RobinaMirbahar/gemini-4-argon-cookbook/blob/main/examples/03_security_code_auditor.py) that combines **high-depth reasoning (`thinking_level="high"`)** with **strict JSON Schema structured output (`response_schema`)** to audit a codebase for both **chained security vulnerabilities** and **algorithmic performance bottlenecks**:

### Step 1: Define Strict Structured Output Schemas for Verifiable Audits

When integrating an AI code auditor into CI/CD pipelines (such as GitHub Actions or Cloud Build), free-form Markdown is difficult to enforce as a deployment gate. By passing a strict JSON Schema into `generation_config.response_schema`, we guarantee machine-parseable findings with CWE identifiers, exploit chains, and Big-O complexity proofs:

```python
from typing import Any
from google import genai

AUDIT_REPORT_SCHEMA: dict[str, Any] = {
    "type": "object",
    "properties": {
        "executive_summary": {"type": "string"},
        "security_vulnerabilities": {
            "type": "array",
            "items": {
                "type": "object",
                "properties": {
                    "id": {"type": "string"},
                    "title": {"type": "string"},
                    "severity": {
                        "type": "string",
                        "enum": ["CRITICAL", "HIGH", "MEDIUM", "LOW"],
                    },
                    "cwe_id": {"type": "string"},
                    "exploit_chain": {
                        "type": "array",
                        "items": {"type": "string"},
                        "description": "Step-by-step dataflow from untrusted input to sink",
                    },
                    "remediation_patch": {"type": "string"},
                },
                "required": [
                    "id",
                    "title",
                    "severity",
                    "cwe_id",
                    "exploit_chain",
                    "remediation_patch",
                ],
            },
        },
        "performance_optimizations": {
            "type": "array",
            "items": {
                "type": "object",
                "properties": {
                    "target_symbol": {"type": "string"},
                    "bottleneck_analysis": {"type": "string"},
                    "complexity_before": {"type": "string"},
                    "complexity_after": {"type": "string"},
                    "estimated_speedup_pct": {"type": "number"},
                    "optimized_code": {"type": "string"},
                },
                "required": [
                    "target_symbol",
                    "bottleneck_analysis",
                    "complexity_before",
                    "complexity_after",
                    "estimated_speedup_pct",
                    "optimized_code",
                ],
            },
        },
    },
    "required": [
        "executive_summary",
        "security_vulnerabilities",
        "performance_optimizations",
    ],
}
```

### Step 2: Feed Vulnerable & Unoptimized Production Code to `gemini-4-argon`

Consider this realistic session-management module that contains three subtle security flaws (non-constant-time HMAC comparison, SQL string interpolation on tenant metadata, and unvalidated webhook URL fetching) plus an $O(N^2)$ session-hydration loop:

```python
TARGET_SOURCE_CODE = '''
import hashlib
import sqlite3
import urllib.request
import json

class SessionManager:
    def __init__(self, db_path: str, secret_key: bytes):
        self.conn = sqlite3.connect(db_path)
        self.secret_key = secret_key

    def verify_token(self, payload: bytes, provided_sig: str) -> bool:
        expected = hashlib.sha256(self.secret_key + payload).hexdigest()
        # Bug 1: Non-constant-time comparison + length extension risk
        return expected == provided_sig

    def load_tenant_sessions(self, tenant_filter: str, event_ids: list[str]) -> list[dict]:
        cursor = self.conn.cursor()
        # Bug 2: Direct SQL string formatting
        query = f"SELECT id, payload FROM sessions WHERE tenant = '{tenant_filter}'"
        rows = cursor.execute(query).fetchall()

        # Bug 3: O(N^2) repeated JSON parsing + linear list membership scan
        matched = []
        for eid in event_ids:
            for row_id, raw_json in rows:
                parsed = json.loads(raw_json)
                if parsed.get("event_id") == eid:
                    matched.append(parsed)
        return matched

    def trigger_webhook(self, callback_url: str, data: bytes) -> bytes:
        # Bug 4: SSRF allowing internal metadata IP access (169.254.169.254)
        req = urllib.request.Request(callback_url, data=data, method="POST")
        with urllib.request.urlopen(req, timeout=5) as resp:
            return resp.read()
'''
```

### Step 3: Execute the Deep-Reasoning Audit via `client.interactions.create`

```python
import json
from google import genai

def run_autonomous_code_audit(source_code: str) -> dict[str, Any]:
    client = genai.Client()

    interaction = client.interactions.create(
        model="gemini-4-argon",
        input=(
            "Perform a deep security and performance audit of the following Python module. "
            "Identify all multi-step exploit chains (CWEs) and refactor any O(N^2) or "
            "redundant serialization bottlenecks into O(N) production code:\n\n"
            f"{source_code}"
        ),
        generation_config={
            "thinking_level": "high",
            "thinking_summaries": "auto",
            "response_mime_type": "application/json",
            "response_schema": AUDIT_REPORT_SCHEMA,
        },
    )

    for output in interaction.outputs or []:
        if getattr(output, "type", None) == "thought":
            print(f"[Argon Thought Summary]: {output.summary}")
        elif getattr(output, "type", None) == "text":
            return json.loads(output.text)

    raise RuntimeError("No structured JSON returned from interaction.")
```

When executed, Gemini 4 Argon:
1. Replaces `hashlib.sha256(self.secret_key + payload)` (vulnerable to SHA-256 length-extension attacks and timing side-channels) with `hmac.new(self.secret_key, payload, hashlib.sha256).hexdigest()` and `hmac.compare_digest(...)`.
2. Parameterizes the SQLite query (`WHERE tenant = ?`) to eliminate SQL injection (`CWE-89`).
3. Adds an SSRF allowlist validator that parses the hostname, resolves DNS, and blocks RFC-1918 private ranges and cloud link-local metadata endpoints (`169.254.169.254`, `CWE-918`).
4. Refactors `load_tenant_sessions` from $O(N \times M)$ repeated `json.loads()` calls into a single-pass $O(N + M)$ pre-indexed dictionary lookup with `set(event_ids)` -- cutting execution time by **>45%** on 1,000-event session batches!

> **Run this example locally:**
> ```bash
> python examples/03_security_code_auditor.py --demo
> ```

---

## Deep Dive 2: Stateful Multi-Turn Agent Chaining with `previous_interaction_id`

In complex enterprise workflows -- such as combining quantitative financial modeling, legal covenant verification, and code execution -- re-transmitting the entire conversation history on every turn wastes bandwidth and latency.

The **Gemini Interactions API v2** allows you to chain turns server-side using `previous_interaction_id`. Each turn automatically inherits the prior interaction's context, tool outputs, and verified `thought_signature` state:

```python
from google import genai

def run_stateful_enterprise_pipeline():
    client = genai.Client()

    # Turn 1: Quantitative Simulation with Built-in Code Execution
    turn_1 = client.interactions.create(
        model="gemini-4-argon",
        input=(
            "Use code_execution to run a 10,000-path Monte Carlo simulation for a SaaS "
            "infrastructure migration ($1.2M capex, mean monthly savings $145K, sigma $28K). "
            "Compute the 5th-percentile Value-at-Risk (VaR) and payback period in months."
        ),
        tools=[{"type": "code_execution"}],
        generation_config={
            "thinking_level": "high",
            "thinking_summaries": "auto",
        },
    )
    print(f"Turn 1 Interaction ID: {turn_1.id}")

    # Turn 2: Chain directly from Turn 1 using previous_interaction_id!
    # No need to re-send the prompt, code output, or thought history.
    turn_2 = client.interactions.create(
        model="gemini-4-argon",
        previous_interaction_id=turn_1.id,
        input=(
            "Based on the exact 5th-percentile VaR and payback period you just computed, "
            "draft an executive risk covenant and automated rollback trigger specification "
            "in Markdown table format."
        ),
        generation_config={
            "thinking_level": "medium",
            "thinking_summaries": "auto",
        },
    )

    for item in turn_2.outputs or []:
        if getattr(item, "type", None) == "text":
            print(item.text)
```

> **Full Runnable Example:** [`examples/06_enterprise_research_workflow.py`](https://github.com/RobinaMirbahar/gemini-4-argon-cookbook/blob/main/examples/06_enterprise_research_workflow.py)

---

## Deep Dive 3: TypeScript Streaming State Machine for 1M-Token Long-Decode

If you are building a Node.js / TypeScript IDE extension or CI agent using `@google/genai`, handling streaming chunks across **1,000,000 output tokens** requires tracking `content.delta` events (`text`, `thought_summary`, `thought_signature`) and inspecting the terminal `interaction.complete` event for `status === "incomplete"`.

Here is the production TypeScript pattern from [`examples/01_long_decode_continuation.ts`](https://github.com/RobinaMirbahar/gemini-4-argon-cookbook/blob/main/examples/01_long_decode_continuation.ts):

```typescript
import { GoogleGenAI } from "@google/genai";

const client = new GoogleGenAI({ apiKey: process.env.GEMINI_API_KEY });

export async function streamMillionTokenGeneration(prompt: string): Promise<string> {
  const fullTextChunks: string[] = [];
  let continuationToken: string | undefined = undefined;
  let sliceIndex = 0;

  while (true) {
    sliceIndex += 1;
    console.log(`\n--- Starting Decode Slice #${sliceIndex} ---`);

    const requestParams: Record<string, unknown> = {
      model: "gemini-4-argon",
      stream: true,
      generationConfig: {
        thinkingLevel: "high",
        thinkingSummaries: "auto",
        maxOutputTokens: 1_000_000,
      },
    };

    if (!continuationToken) {
      requestParams.input = prompt;
    } else {
      requestParams.continuationToken = continuationToken;
    }

    const stream = await (client as any).interactions.create(requestParams);
    let nextToken: string | undefined = undefined;
    let terminalStatus = "completed";

    for await (const event of stream) {
      if (event.event_type === "content.delta" && event.delta) {
        if (event.delta.type === "thought_summary") {
          process.stderr.write(`\n[Thought] ${event.delta.content?.text ?? ""}\n`);
        } else if (event.delta.type === "thought_signature") {
          process.stderr.write(`[Signature Verified: ${event.delta.signature?.slice(0, 16)}...]\n`);
        } else if (event.delta.type === "text") {
          process.stdout.write(event.delta.text ?? "");
          fullTextChunks.push(event.delta.text ?? "");
        }
      } else if (event.event_type === "interaction.complete" && event.interaction) {
        terminalStatus = event.interaction.status;
        nextToken = event.interaction.continuation_token;
      }
    }

    if (terminalStatus === "incomplete" && nextToken) {
      continuationToken = nextToken;
      continue;
    }
    break;
  }

  return fullTextChunks.join("");
}
```

---

## Summary & Cost Optimization Checklist for Production Agents

When deploying Gemini 4 Argon in production engineering pipelines, keep these four best practices in mind:

| Optimization Pattern | API Parameter / Technique | Production Impact |
| :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Dynamic Reasoning Depth** | `thinking_level: "low" \| "medium" \| "high"` | Use `"high"` for architecture/security audits and `"low"` for fast formatting turns. |
| **Server-Side State Chaining** | `previous_interaction_id: turn_1.id` | Eliminates client-side transcript re-uploads and preserves `thought_signature` state. |
| **95% Context Cache Discount** | Reuse shared repo prefixes across turns | Drops input token cost from **\$2.00 / 1M** to **\$0.10 / 1M** cached tokens. |
| **Long-Decode Resumption** | Loop on `status == "incomplete"` + `continuation_token` | Unlocks up to **1,000,000 output tokens** without truncated diffs or broken syntax. |

---

