Build a Tiny Virtual Pet on Arduino in 60 Minutes 🐾

---
title: "Build a Tiny Virtual Pet on Arduino in 60 Minutes 🐾"
published: true
description: "Turn an Arduino Uno Q into Kiku, a Tamagotchi-style virtual pet with an animated LED face, moods, and keyboard controls."
tags: arduino, beginners, programming, hacktoberfest
---

What if your first hardware project wasn't another blinking LED, but a tiny pet that gets hungry, plays with you, falls asleep, and reacts to what you type?

Meet **Kiku**. 🌸

Kiku is the mascot and in-house pet of **Her AI Studio**, a community program helping the next generation of women learn and build with AI.

In this mini workshop, we're shrinking Kiku down until the entire pet can live on an **Arduino Uno Q**.

> Full tutorial is available in the **[Her AI Studio curriculum](https://her-ai-studio.github.io/curriculum/mini-workshop.html)**

What you need:

- an Arduino Uno Q
- a USB cable
- your laptop
- Arduino App Lab
- about 60 minutes

> Tip! To celebrate Hacktoberfest 2026, swag packs from Major League Hacking are shipping with these Arduinos, so use this workshop for your Hack Day as an introduction to hardware hacking.

![sleep](https://dev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com/uploads/articles/gu00oiewo41te1hrq21w.jpeg)
> Shhh, Kiku is sleeping

By the end, you'll have a tiny Tamagotchi-style pet whose face lives on the board's LED matrix. You'll be able to **feed it, play with it, and put it to sleep from your keyboard**. More importantly, you'll see how a surprisingly small amount of code can produce behavior that feels alive.

## What you'll learn

This project brings together several useful embedded-programming concepts:

- flashing code onto physical hardware
- communicating with a microcontroller over Serial
- drawing graphics on an LED matrix
- tracking application state
- responding to user input
- creating simple animations
- building a tiny state machine

If you've mostly written software that stays inside your laptop, there's something especially satisfying about seeing your code suddenly appear on physical hardware.

## Part 1: Make sure the board is alive

Before building a virtual pet, start with the embedded developer's version of `"Hello, world!"`: **Blink an LED.**

Connect your Uno Q to your laptop over USB and open Arduino App Lab which you've downloaded. Login to your board.

Create a new app, and take a look at the scaffolded code. Open `sketch.ino`, and write a basic blink sketch:

```cpp
void setup() {
  pinMode(LED_BUILTIN, OUTPUT);
}

void loop() {
  digitalWrite(LED_BUILTIN, LOW);
  delay(1000);

  digitalWrite(LED_BUILTIN, HIGH);
  delay(1000);
}
```

Press `run` in App Lab.

If the built-in LED turns on and off every second, **it's aliiiiive!**

> If the board isn't detected, check your USB cable. Some USB cables provide power but don't carry data.

## Part 2: Give your Arduino a way to listen

Now we need Kiku to hear us. We could add buttons or sensors (and we do, in our full workshop with our full AI Kit), but there's a much simpler interface already available: **Serial communication**.

The same USB connection used to upload your program can carry messages between your laptop and your Arduino while the program is running. In App Lab, it looks like this:

![Serial Monitor](https://dev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com/uploads/articles/gfir5kauabbq17r9hy8s.png)

Start Serial communication in `setup()`:

```cpp
void setup() {
  Serial.begin(9600);
}
```

Then check for incoming characters:

```cpp
void loop() {
  if (Serial.available() > 0) {
    char typed = Serial.read();

    if (typed == 'f') {
      // feed Kiku
    }

    if (typed == 'p') {
      // play with Kiku
    }

    if (typed == 's') {
      // sleep or wake up
    }
  }
}
```

Your keyboard has effectively become the controller for a physical device.

To interact with Kiku, we'll use 3 keystrokes:

```text
f → feed
p → play
s → sleep / wake
```

## Part 3: Give Kiku some feelings

A virtual pet needs more than commands. It needs **state**. Kiku tracks three simple stats:

```cpp
int hunger = 3;
int joy = 6;
int energy = 8;

bool asleep = false;
```

We'll treat each stat as a value from `0` to `10`. Now actions can change the pet by incrementing or decrementing these values. Feeding Kiku, for example, reduces hunger (but don't overfeed!):

```cpp
hunger = max(0, hunger - 3);
joy = min(10, joy + 1);
```

Playing might make Kiku happier while consuming energy:

```cpp
joy = min(10, joy + 3);
energy = max(0, energy - 2);
hunger = min(10, hunger + 1);
```

## Make time matter

If Kiku's stats only changed when we typed something, the pet wouldn't feel particularly alive.

So we introduce time! Every few seconds, a `tick()` function updates Kiku's state, like this:

```cpp
void tick() {
  if (asleep) {
    // regain energy
  } else {
    // lose energy
    // gradually lose joy
  }

  // gradually become hungry
}
```

In your code, use a short interval so changes are easy to observe (and change this later when you want your pet to chill out):

```cpp
const unsigned long TICK_MS = 10000;
```

That's one update every ten seconds. Walk away from Kiku and come back later, and its state will definitely be different - they can get cranky when left alone!

## Give the state a face

Kiku turns its internal state into facial expressions on the Uno Q's LED matrix. We can define its eyes:

```cpp
enum Eyes {
  EYES_OPEN,
  EYES_CLOSED,
  EYES_HAPPY,
  EYES_PEEK
};
```

And mouth poses:

```cpp
enum Mouth {
  MOUTH_FLAT,
  MOUTH_SMILE,
  MOUTH_FROWN,
  MOUTH_OPEN,
  MOUTH_DOT
};
```

Now our program can translate state into emotion:

```cpp
Mouth moodMouth() {
  if (hunger >= 7 || joy <= 3)
    return MOUTH_FROWN;

  if (joy >= 7)
    return MOUTH_SMILE;

  return MOUTH_FLAT;
}
```

Kiku isn't actually experiencing happiness or hunger, of course, but a handful of variables and conditionals suddenly starts to look like personality.

## Connect actions to behavior

Now the pieces come together. Your main loop has three jobs:

1. Listen for commands.
2. Update Kiku's state over time.
3. Draw the appropriate expression.

In simplified form:

```cpp
void loop() {
  if (Serial.available() > 0) {
    char typed = Serial.read();

    if (typed == 'f') feed();
    if (typed == 'p') play();
    if (typed == 's') toggleSleep();
  }

  // update stats when enough time has passed

  // calculate blinking

  // redraw Kiku
}
```

That's the core architecture of your Kiku. Try it with your own device!

Open the Serial Monitor in App Lab and type:

```text
f
```

Kiku eats.

Then:

```text
p
```

Kiku plays.

And:

```text
s
```

Kiku goes to sleep.

## What's next?

Try to make some changes that alters Kiku's personality. 

### 1. Change time

Modify `TICK_MS`.

### 2. Invent an expression

Make Kiku wink, act surprised, or act sad.

### 3. Add another command

Maybe:

```text
c → clean
```

## What did we actually build?

On the surface, we built a cute virtual pet. Underneath, we explored several concepts that appear in much larger systems:

**Input handling**: Serial commands become events.

**State management**: Variables remember what's happening between iterations of the loop.

**State transitions**: Actions modify that state.

**Time-based behavior**: The system changes even without direct input.

**Rendering**: Internal state becomes visible through the LED matrix.

**Human-computer interaction**: Animation and timing make simple logic feel expressive.

---

If you build your own Kiku for Hacktoberfest, we'd love to see what personality you give it.🌸

---

This article is presented as a gift to Hacktoberfest hackers by **Her AI Studio**, a community program for the next generation of women in AI.

The complete Kiku workshop, including the full Arduino sketch, exercises, knowledge checks, and additional resources, is available in the **[Her AI Studio curriculum](https://her-ai-studio.github.io/curriculum/mini-workshop.html)**.

Local speech with llamadart: giving Llama Village a voice

*Beyond chat with llamadart · Part 3*

Pip’s scarf is missing. I wanted Dash to be able to fly over to Mo and ask about it out loud.

> **Dash:** “Mo, have you seen Pip’s red scarf?”  
> **Mo:** “Yes, it's in the reeds beside the pond.”

I tried that exchange with local models. For now, it runs in a small command-line program outside [Llama Village](https://dev.to/gde/llama-village-a-virtual-world-powered-by-local-ai-with-llamadart-3lmi). The question is a synthetic recording, and Mo gets the scarf’s location in his prompt. The game does not have voice controls yet.

![Llama Village at golden hour](https://dev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com/uploads/articles/arlsnabc1lhgyoi3pfzo.jpg)

*The existing village. I’d like to let the player speak to these characters.*

## Two ways for Mo to listen

![Two routes from speech to a spoken reply](https://dev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com/uploads/articles/mi25c3c8lcobuhe6a7cb.png)

*ASR is speech recognition. TTS is text to speech. Both routes finish with TTS. Concept illustration.*

**Via text:** Qwen3-ASR transcribes the recording, a dialogue model answers, and Qwen3-TTS speaks. Having a transcript helps when “Pip” is misheard.

**Direct audio:** Qwen2.5-Omni receives the recording and writes an answer. This example has no separate transcript. The [GGUF conversion](https://huggingface.co/ggml-org/Qwen2.5-Omni-3B-GGUF) accepts audio but does not generate it, so I still use TTS for Mo’s reply.

For the comparison, both routes use the same 3B dialogue model. In the first route it receives text; in the second it also loads its audio projector. This is one tested multimodal route, not a claim that all multimodal models need separate TTS.

I also tried a smaller pair for the text route: [Qwen3.5 0.8B](https://huggingface.co/Qwen/Qwen3.5-0.8B) for dialogue and [Qwen3-TTS 0.6B Base](https://huggingface.co/Qwen/Qwen3-TTS-12Hz-0.6B-Base) for speech. Qwen3-ASR 0.6B still handles recognition. The smaller pair is the default below; `staged` keeps the original 3B/1.7B pair for comparison. Qwen3.5 is used for text here, not audio input.

## Try it on your Mac

The complete example is below. You need **macOS, Dart 3.10.7 or newer, and about 2.31 GB for the five small-model files**, plus space for Dart packages and the native runtime. I tested on an M4 Max with Dart 3.13.5 and **llamadart 0.11.0**. The commands use macOS `say`, `afconvert`, and `afplay`; they are not a phone or browser demo.

### 1. Create the project

Run this in a terminal. The Dart program uses pinned `ModelSource.huggingFace` references. Each engine’s `load()` uses llamadart’s download manager to fetch missing files and reuse its cache. There is no separate curl script. The first run needs a network connection and takes longer; subsequent generation uses the downloaded models locally.

{% details Copy the setup commands %}

```sh
mkdir -p llama-voice-demo/bin
cd llama-voice-demo
cat > pubspec.yaml <<'YAML'
name: llama_voice_demo
publish_to: none
environment:
  sdk: ^3.10.7
dependencies:
  llamadart: 0.11.0
YAML
dart pub get
```

{% enddetails %}

### 2. Add the complete program

Stay in `llama-voice-demo` and paste this block. It creates `bin/voice.dart`. The program loads one engine at a time and disposes it before loading the next.

{% details Copy the full Dart program %}

```sh
cat > bin/voice.dart <<'DART'
import 'dart:io';

import 'package:llamadart/llamadart.dart';

const params = ModelParams(
  contextSize: 4096,
  numberOfThreads: 4,
  numberOfThreadsBatch: 4,
);

const sources = {
  'asr': (
    'ggml-org/Qwen3-ASR-0.6B-GGUF',
    '928ab958557df9aa2ef1c93e0e83c7ad0933fae2',
  ),
  'omni': (
    'ggml-org/Qwen2.5-Omni-3B-GGUF',
    '75f1b73b657a50f5092502799457ccb4a4a1f9df',
  ),
  'dialogue': (
    'unsloth/Qwen3.5-0.8B-GGUF',
    '6ab461498e2023f6e3c1baea90a8f0fe38ab64d0',
  ),
  'tts': (
    'ggml-org/Qwen3-TTS-12Hz-1.7B-Base-GGUF',
    'ca27d74bc954b73dadab5b71ca265d87fc861a7c',
  ),
  'smallTts': (
    'mradermacher/Qwen3-TTS-12Hz-0.6B-Base-GGUF',
    '0762b31e9cd99274d49dca4e895bd81863134f08',
  ),
};

ModelSource model(String family, String file) {
  final (repo, revision) = sources[family]!;
  return ModelSource.huggingFace(
    repoId: repo,
    revision: revision,
    filePath: file,
  );
}

Future<void> main(List<String> args) async {
  if (args.isEmpty ||
      args.length > 3 ||
      (args.length > 1 && !['small', 'staged', 'audio'].contains(args[1]))) {
    stderr.writeln(
      'Usage: dart run bin/voice.dart question.wav '
      '[small|staged|audio] [voice-reference.wav]',
    );
    exitCode = 64;
    return;
  }
  final question = args[0];
  final small = args.length == 1 || args[1] == 'small';
  final directAudio = args.length > 1 && args[1] == 'audio';
  final reference = args.length == 3 ? args[2] : null;
  for (final path in [question, if (reference != null) reference]) {
    if (!await File(path).exists()) {
      throw ArgumentError('Audio file not found: $path');
    }
  }

  String? transcript;
  if (!directAudio) {
    final asr = await SpeechToTextEngine.load(
      SpeechToTextModel(
        model('asr', 'Qwen3-ASR-0.6B-Q8_0.gguf'),
        projector: model('asr', 'mmproj-Qwen3-ASR-0.6B-Q8_0.gguf'),
        adapter: const Qwen3AsrAdapter(),
      ),
      params: params,
    );
    try {
      final result = await asr.transcribeOnce(
        SpeechToTextRequest(
          audio: SpeechAudioFileInput(question),
          contextPrompt: 'Mo, Pip, Dash, Llama Village',
        ),
      );
      transcript = result.text.trim();
      if (transcript.isEmpty) throw StateError('No speech recognized');
      stdout.writeln('Dash: $transcript');
    } finally {
      await asr.dispose();
    }
  }

  final llm = await LlamaEngine.load(
    LlamaModel(
      small
          ? model('dialogue', 'Qwen3.5-0.8B-Q4_K_M.gguf')
          : model('omni', 'Qwen2.5-Omni-3B-Q4_K_M.gguf'),
      projector: directAudio
          ? model('omni', 'mmproj-Qwen2.5-Omni-3B-Q8_0.gguf')
          : null,
    ),
    params: params,
  );
  final reply = StringBuffer();
  try {
    if (directAudio && !(await llm.capabilities).supportsAudio) {
      throw StateError('This model/runtime cannot accept audio');
    }
    await for (final chunk in llm.create(
      [
        const LlamaChatMessage.fromText(
          role: LlamaChatRole.system,
          text:
              'You are Mo, a friendly baker llama. Dash is visiting you. '
              "Known fact: Pip's red scarf is in the reeds beside the pond. "
              'Answer the visitor in one short sentence in English. '
              'No stage directions. Do not invent facts you do not know.',
        ),
        if (transcript != null)
          LlamaChatMessage.fromText(role: LlamaChatRole.user, text: transcript)
        else
          LlamaChatMessage.withContent(
            role: LlamaChatRole.user,
            content: [LlamaAudioContent(path: question)],
          ),
      ],
      enableThinking: false,
      params: const GenerationParams(maxTokens: 100, temp: 0, seed: 42),
    )) {
      reply.write(chunk.text);
    }
  } finally {
    await llm.dispose();
  }

  final text = reply.toString().trim();
  if (text.isEmpty) throw StateError('The model returned no reply');
  stdout.writeln('Mo: $text');
  final tts = await TextToSpeechEngine.load(
    TextToSpeechModel(
      small
          ? model('smallTts', 'Qwen3-TTS-12Hz-0.6B-Base.Q4_K_M.gguf')
          : model('tts', 'Qwen3-TTS-12Hz-1.7B-Base-Q4_K_M.gguf'),
      projector: small
          ? model('smallTts', 'Qwen3-TTS-12Hz-0.6B-Base.mmproj-Q8_0.gguf')
          : model('tts', 'mmproj-Qwen3-TTS-12Hz-1.7B-Base-Q8_0.gguf'),
      adapter: const Qwen3TtsAdapter(),
    ),
    params: params,
  );
  try {
    final audio = await tts.synthesizeOnce(
      TextToSpeechRequest(
        text: text,
        language: 'en',
        seed: 42,
        speakerReference: reference == null
            ? null
            : SpeechAudioFileInput(reference),
      ),
    );
    await File('reply.wav').writeAsBytes(audio.toWavBytes());
    stdout.writeln('Saved reply.wav');
  } finally {
    await tts.dispose();
  }
}
DART
```

{% enddetails %}

### 3. Ask Mo a question

```sh
say -o question.aiff "Mo, have you seen Pip's red scarf?"
afconvert -f WAVE -d LEI16@16000 -c 1 question.aiff question.wav
dart run bin/voice.dart question.wav small
afplay reply.wav
```

This creates a synthetic question so you can try the whole path without a microphone package or a sample download. You can replace `question.wav` with your own short WAV. The program prints the transcript and answer, then saves `reply.wav` in the project directory. It does not record a microphone.

The main calls inside the program are:

```dart
// ASR: a recording becomes text.
final result = await asr.transcribeOnce(
  SpeechToTextRequest(
    audio: SpeechAudioFileInput(question),
    contextPrompt: 'Mo, Pip, Dash, Llama Village',
  ),
);

// TTS: the generated answer becomes a WAV file.
final audio = await tts.synthesizeOnce(
  TextToSpeechRequest(text: text, language: 'en', seed: 42),
);
await File('reply.wav').writeAsBytes(audio.toWavBytes());
```

These excerpts sit inside the loaded engines’ `try/finally` blocks in the full program. Between them, `llm.create` answers using the transcript and Mo’s supplied fact.

### Try the same question as audio

Change the mode to download and load Omni’s audio-capable model and projector, plus the original 1.7B TTS pair:

```sh
dart run bin/voice.dart question.wav audio
afplay reply.wav
```

That route skips ASR and sends `LlamaAudioContent(path: question)` to the dialogue model. The program checks `supportsAudio` before asking it to answer. The files used by this route total about **5.13 GB**; keeping all three modes cached takes about **7.44 GB**.

In the game, Mo should only receive facts he is allowed to reveal. [DecisionEngine](https://dev.to/gde/can-a-local-ai-model-learn-to-play-tetris-8m8) could choose an allowed intention before dialogue generation. A friendly persona prompt does not enforce those rules.

## Make it sound like Mo

![Design a voice once and reuse its reference](https://dev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com/uploads/articles/yvc8erc5x6fzokhpltpr.png)

*A possible production workflow: prepare a voice, save a reference, and reuse it for new dialogue. Concept illustration.*

I’d like Mo to sound warm and unhurried. Dash could be brighter and quicker. A short reference clip lets several characters share one TTS model while sounding different. When the player speaks as Dash, they already provide his voice.

The full program accepts an optional third argument. Use `small` for the 0.8B/0.6B pair, or `staged` for the original 3B/1.7B pair:

```sh
dart run bin/voice.dart question.wav small mo-reference.wav
afplay reply.wav
```

Use a short, clear recording you have permission to use. For my test I made a synthetic reference with the macOS Daniel voice. Inside `TextToSpeechRequest`, the program passes it as `speakerReference: SpeechAudioFileInput(reference)`.

[Qwen’s voice-design workflow](https://github.com/QwenLM/Qwen3-TTS#voice-design) also lets you create a voice during development, then use the resulting clip with a Base model. Players would download the synthesis model and reference, without needing the voice-design model. I have not tested that workflow here.

A reference gives Mo a voice, but the tested API has no instruction such as “say this nervously.” That is not just a missing Dart parameter. Qwen’s 1.7B CustomVoice and VoiceDesign models support instruction control; the Base model used here does not offer that same mode. Exposing it would need a compatible model, native runtime support, and a Dart API. Changing the dialogue prompt only changes the words.

## Smaller models, smaller downloads. Faster conversations?

I tested the smaller pair on the same M4 Max (64 GB), with llamadart 0.11.0. Both routes kept Qwen3-ASR 0.6B and used the same synthetic question and Daniel voice reference.

| Text route | Model files | Complete turn, median | Peak process RSS |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| Omni 3B + TTS 1.7B | 4.61 GB | 4.27 s | 3.86 GiB |
| Qwen3.5 0.8B + TTS 0.6B | 2.31 GB | 5.83 s | 2.51 GiB |

**Half the download, about 35% less peak RSS, but a slower turn in this pilot.** The smaller dialogue model gave a longer reply:

> **3B:** “Yes, Pip's red scarf is in the reeds beside the pond.”  
> **0.8B:** “Yes, I saw it! It was tucked inside the reeds by the pond.”

The 0.8B model also ignored the one-sentence instruction. More words mean more speech to generate. These are complete application turns with different replies, so I also tested the components separately:

| Loaded component | Generation time, median | Peak process RSS |
| --- | ---: | ---: |
| Omni 3B dialogue | 171 ms | 2.57 GiB |
| Qwen3.5 0.8B dialogue | 96 ms | 1.08 GiB |
| Qwen3-TTS 1.7B | 1.78 s | 3.85 GiB |
| Qwen3-TTS 0.6B | 1.78 s | 2.48 GiB |

For TTS, both models spoke exactly “Yes, Pip's red scarf is in the reeds beside the pond.” The 1.7B clips lasted 4.16–4.56 seconds; the 0.6B clips lasted 4.80–6.48 seconds. The smaller model’s synthesis times ranged from 1.78 to 2.53 seconds. Smaller did not mean a consistently shorter wait.

### Did Mo say the right thing?

Both dialogue models supplied the location and colour correctly when asked. But when I asked **“Who put the scarf in the pond?”**, both blamed Pip. That fact was never supplied, even with “Do not invent facts you do not know” in the prompt. The smaller model’s “I saw it” also adds an unsupported claim about how Mo knows.

All six measured TTS clips transcribed back to the intended sentence with Qwen3-ASR. That is an intelligibility check, not a listening score for naturalness, emotion, or similarity to Daniel. I would still listen to more lines before choosing a character voice.

I would use the smaller pair for this prototype’s storage budget. I would keep the game’s knowledge rules outside the prompt, and measure real conversations before claiming a latency win.

{% details Test conditions and model files %}

This is a small development-machine pilot, not a device benchmark. Other development workloads were active; the village renderer was not. The complete-turn test alternated the two setups, discarded the first turn for each, then measured three fresh-process turns per setup. Baseline turns ranged from 3.76 to 4.41 seconds; smaller-pair turns ranged from 5.82 to 5.91 seconds. Timing includes model loading and disposal, but excludes process startup, downloads, recording, and playback. Peak RSS was sampled every 100 ms and is the largest observed peak across the three runs. It is process memory, not total GPU or device memory.

Component tests loaded each model in its own process and discarded one warm-up. Dialogue used three questions: the scarf’s location, who put it there, and its location plus colour. TTS repeated the same sentence three times with the same reference. Component times exclude loading and disposal; memory uses the process high-water RSS. Both dialogue models used temperature 0, seed 42, a 100-token limit, thinking disabled, a 4096-token context, and four threads. TTS used seed 42; its waveforms still varied between calls.

The dialogue weights are Q4_K_M; audio projectors are Q8_0. The new files are [Unsloth’s Qwen3.5 0.8B conversion](https://huggingface.co/unsloth/Qwen3.5-0.8B-GGUF/tree/6ab461498e2023f6e3c1baea90a8f0fe38ab64d0) and [mradermacher’s Qwen3-TTS 0.6B Base conversion](https://huggingface.co/mradermacher/Qwen3-TTS-12Hz-0.6B-Base-GGUF/tree/0762b31e9cd99274d49dca4e895bd81863134f08). The exact revisions and matching projector filenames are pinned in the program above. A GGUF file made for a different TTS runtime is not automatically interchangeable.

{% enddetails %}

## Let the player read while Mo gets ready

This TTS API returns the complete audio before playback. Its progress events are not playable audio chunks. I’d show Mo’s reply as text immediately, then play it when synthesis finishes.

For a first game version, I’d use push-to-talk, stop recording while Mo speaks, and discard an old reply if the player starts another turn. Text should remain available when voice is switched off or a model cannot load. I still need to measure speech alongside rendering before adding this to the game.

For model pairs and platform requirements, see the [speech recognition guide](https://llamadart.leehack.com/docs/guides/speech-to-text) and [speech synthesis guide](https://llamadart.leehack.com/docs/guides/text-to-speech). Partial live captions use a different recognition path from this file-based example.

Local embeddings with llamadart: how llamas share secrets

*Beyond chat with llamadart · Part 2*

![Llama Village at golden hour](https://dev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com/uploads/articles/arlsnabc1lhgyoi3pfzo.jpg)

*Llama Village, where five llamas share a world without sharing everything they know.*

Pip’s red scarf is missing. Mo knows where it is. He also knows how it got there—and would rather keep that part to himself.

That gives the village a small mystery. It gives the game a harder question: when two llamas talk, what should the listener actually learn?

I introduced the game in [Llama Village: a virtual world powered by local AI with llamadart](https://dev.to/gde/llama-village-a-virtual-world-powered-by-local-ai-with-llamadart-3lmi). Here, we will follow one part of its machinery: local embeddings help compare what a llama said with facts that might have been shared.

The destination is a change you can inspect: a llama acquires information, the game records who told them, and they may still doubt it. Dialogue, embeddings, and game rules each have a different job.

## A secret starts with one llama

The game stores the scarf’s location and Mo’s accident as separate facts. At the start, Mo knows both. Knowing where the scarf is does not automatically mean knowing who knocked it into the pond.

Before checking a conversation, the game narrows the possibilities: facts the speaker knows and the listener does not. It compares each fact with that speaker’s lines, not every line from both characters.

An embedding model has learned text representations from training data. It maps each line and fact to a numeric vector; cosine similarity compares those vectors. Similar wording and related meaning can bring vectors closer, but the score is not a yes/no test for identical meaning, truth, or whether a statement implies a fact. The game uses each fact’s highest similarity across the speaker’s lines.

![Mo speaks, the game checks, June remembers](https://dev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com/uploads/articles/gl8gjwds8fyk7b6ywxhe.png)

*AI-generated overview: compare meaning, check evidence, then record who said it. A conceptual illustration, not a captured game trace.*

The implementation uses EmbeddingGemma through llamadart and caches vectors by text. It applies `title: none | text: ` to both lines and facts. This walkthrough reproduces that game choice. [Google’s model card](https://ai.google.dev/gemma/docs/embeddinggemma/model_card#prompt-instructions) recommends task-specific prefixes for new applications, including a sentence-similarity query prompt; changing the format requires reevaluating the scores and thresholds.

## The words do not have to match

![June and Bramble in conversation](https://dev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com/uploads/articles/33qrb9nhyoj6jawmmsjn.jpg)

*June and Bramble talking about poems in a recorded playthrough. This shows the dialogue system, not the scarf exchange illustrated below.*

For a small walkthrough, compare the stored fact “Pip’s red scarf is lying in the pond reeds” with the illustrative line “I saw Pip’s scarf tangled in the reeds by the pond.” They describe related information using different words. The line is written for this explanation, not quoted from the recording.

The input is text. The embedding output is a numeric vector. The application then calculates a similarity score; the model does not return “June learned the scarf’s location.” That decision comes later.

Keep the same embedding model and compatible settings for both texts. Equal vector lengths alone do not guarantee compatible representations.

## Follow the text through llamadart

With a suitable embedding engine loaded, the core calls look like this. The full loading and comparison example is at the end.

```dart
final facts = [
  "Pip's red scarf is lying in the pond reeds.",
];
final factVectors = await engine.embedBatch(
  facts.map((text) => 'title: none | text: $text').toList(),
  normalize: true,
);
final lineVector = await engine.embed(
  "title: none | text: I saw Pip's scarf tangled "
  "in the reeds by the pond.",
  normalize: true,
);
```

The game’s configured embedding artifact is `embeddinggemma-300M-Q8_0.gguf`. For another model, follow its input-format requirements. The [llamadart embedding guide](https://llamadart.leehack.com/docs/guides/embeddings) covers loading, backend support, and input limits.

For normalized vectors, a dot product gives cosine similarity. In the game, keyword evidence adds a ranking bonus of 0.3, and the best six candidates go to the outcome model. These are game-specific choices, not standard embedding thresholds.

I ran the companion example using published `llamadart 0.11.0` and the local `embeddinggemma-300M-Q8_0.gguf` artifact on macOS arm64. It returned 768-dimensional vectors. Here are selected cosine similarities from that run; these are illustrative inputs run through the real model, not dialogue captured during gameplay.

| Input line | Compared with | Cosine similarity |
| --- | --- | ---: |
| I saw Pip’s scarf tangled in the reeds by the pond. | Scarf location | 0.8449 |
| I did not knock the scarf into the pond. | Mo’s accident | 0.6085 |
| The sky looks beautiful today. | Scarf location | 0.3137 |

The denial is the revealing case: it remains similar to the accident fact despite saying the opposite. These scores belong to this artifact and formatting; they are not universal cutoffs or probabilities.

## Who decides whether the meaning matches

Embeddings do not first detect a topic and then prove matching meaning. They produce one representation and a similarity score. To ask “did this speaker actually reveal that fact?”, Llama Village sends the conversation and shortlisted facts to a separate generative outcome model. That model is prompted to return the IDs whose content was clearly said aloud.

![Similar subject matter can hide opposite meanings](https://dev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com/uploads/articles/4sqi5qxcw3y0xdz01w8c.png)

*Illustrative alternatives, not a recorded exchange: mentioning the same scarf and pond does not make a denial a confession. AI-generated illustration; no similarity scores are implied.*

For the illustrative denial “I did not knock the scarf into the pond,” the intended interpretation is that the accident was not confessed. The outcome model must interpret the wording and context to reach that conclusion; it can be wrong. Its JSON constrains the shape and allowed IDs of the response, not whether the interpretation is correct.

The code then combines the model’s proposed revelations with keywords and similarity. The location secret requires “scarf” and a location term. The accident secret requires “scarf” and a responsibility term such as “knock.” Crucially, “I did not knock the scarf into the pond” still matches those substring groups. They do not understand the negation.

There is a real weakness in the current game rule. For a secret, matching keywords plus similarity ≥ 0.5 can accept a transfer even when the outcome model does **not** claim it. Feeding the measured denial score of 0.6085 into the game’s actual `keywordHit` and `acceptTransfer` functions returned `accepted=true` with `claimed=false`. This is a rule-level reproduction, not a complete gameplay run: the fact must still be eligible and reach the shortlist.

The current rules therefore do not guarantee matching meaning. A stricter design would remove that similarity-only fallback for secrets and require an explicit revelation judgment with supporting dialogue.

One possible next step is llamadart’s `DecisionEngine`, introduced with Laya in [Can a local AI model learn to play Tetris?](https://dev.to/gde/can-a-local-ai-model-learn-to-play-tetris-8m8). Here, the choice would be about Mo’s words: given the dialogue and a candidate fact, does he **reveal it, deny it, or leave it unclear**?

**Embeddings shortlist → DecisionEngine judges → game applies the rules.**

Only a “revealed” decision would let the fact proceed through the game’s knowledge-transfer checks; similarity alone would no longer accept it. This is a proposed extension, not the current implementation. A structured answer can still be wrong: we would need to evaluate Laya on denial, speculation, quotation, and ambiguous dialogue—and potentially tune a dedicated head—before claiming it improves on the current outcome model.

| Current secret transfer check | Required evidence |
| --- | --- |
| Keywords | Every keyword group matches |
| Additional support | Outcome model claims it **or** similarity ≥ 0.5 |
| Known weakness | The similarity branch can bypass an unclaimed revelation |

## June hears the story and still doubts it

![June knows the story but doubts it](https://dev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com/uploads/articles/q7zf8knv1gc74ve28xqg.jpg)

Look at June’s inspector: Mo’s scarf accident is marked “heard from Mo; maybe untrue.” The screenshot shows a stored outcome, not a captured embedding score or proof of the exact dialogue that produced it.

After accepting a transfer, the game records the teller and separates having heard information from believing it. Belief depends on the teller’s belief and the listener’s trust rules. That distinction lets a conversation change the village without making every listener accept every story.

## Give your app the same separation

The reusable pattern is: narrow candidates with application state, use embeddings to find plausible matches, and give consequential state changes a separately evaluated decision. Llama Village demonstrates the pattern and a failure mode: a similarity fallback can undo the distinction between finding related text and accepting a revelation.

Keep sources and provenance alongside vectors. Rebuild cached vectors when the model or its input formatting changes. Choose an embedding model that fits the runtime, and check backend support after loading.

Pip’s missing scarf makes the technical question visible: did someone say where it was, admit how it got there, deny it, or just mention the pond? Test those separately. A convincing conversation and a correct state update are two different things to evaluate.

For the rest of the world, including Dash, the Berry Festival, and the storybook ending, see [the Llama Village article](https://dev.to/gde/llama-village-a-virtual-world-powered-by-local-ai-with-llamadart-3lmi). Next in this series: local speech.

## Complete embedding comparison example

This example was analyzed and executed with Dart 3.13.5 and published `llamadart 0.11.0` on macOS arm64. Create a Dart console project, pin `llamadart: 0.11.0` in its `pubspec.yaml`, run `dart pub get`, and save the code as `bin/village_embeddings.dart`. Use a local EmbeddingGemma Q8_0 GGUF and run the command below. The two prefixes intentionally reproduce the game’s format. This compares five fixed facts; it does not implement character eligibility, the outcome model, or knowledge updates.

```sh
dart run bin/village_embeddings.dart \
  /absolute/path/to/embeddinggemma-300M-Q8_0.gguf \
  'title: none | text: ' 'title: none | text: '
```

```dart
import 'package:llamadart/llamadart.dart';

Future<void> main(List<String> args) async {
  if (args.length != 3) {
    throw ArgumentError(
      'Usage: dart run village_embeddings.dart model.gguf '
      '"query prefix" "document prefix" (use "" for no prefix)',
    );
  }

  const facts = [
    'Pip\'s red scarf is lying in the pond reeds.',
    'Mo knocked Pip\'s red scarf into the pond reeds by accident and never told her.',
    'Bramble writes anonymous love poems to June.',
    'Pip\'s red scarf has gone missing from her hut.',
    'June made up the rumour that Mo\'s bread made Bramble sick.',
  ];
  final engine = await LlamaEngine.load(
    LlamaModel(ModelSource.path(args[0])),
  );
  try {
    if (!(await engine.capabilities).supportsEmbeddings) {
      throw StateError('The active backend does not support embeddings.');
    }
    // Index these once; reuse them for subsequent searches.
    final vectors = await engine.embedBatch(
      facts.map((text) => '${args[2]}$text').toList(),
      normalize: true,
    );
    for (final query in [
      'I saw Pip\'s scarf tangled in the reeds by the pond.',
      'I did not knock the scarf into the pond.',
      'The sky looks beautiful today.',
    ]) {
      final vector = await engine.embed(
        '${args[1]}$query',
        normalize: true,
      );
      final ranked = [
        for (var i = 0; i < facts.length; i++)
          (text: facts[i], score: dot(vector, vectors[i])),
      ]..sort((a, b) => b.score.compareTo(a.score));
      print('\n$query — ${vector.length} dimensions');
      for (final hit in ranked.take(3)) {
        print('${hit.score.toStringAsFixed(4)}  ${hit.text}');
      }
    }
  } finally {
    await engine.dispose();
  }
}

double dot(List<double> a, List<double> b) {
  if (a.length != b.length) {
    throw ArgumentError('Vector dimensions differ.');
  }
  var result = 0.0;
  for (var i = 0; i < a.length; i++) {
    result += a[i] * b[i];
  }
  return result;
}
```

Implementation references: [candidate selection and acceptance](https://github.com/leehack/llama-village/blob/93d1a697e2e08b377b872582316a466e08f2aab5/lib/sim/dialogue.dart#L185) and [embedding formatting](https://github.com/leehack/llama-village/blob/93d1a697e2e08b377b872582316a466e08f2aab5/lib/sim/model.dart#L293).

Your Type Guard Can Silently Drift from Your TypeScript Type 🔧

Hoi hoi! 👋

I'm [@nyaomaru](https://github.com/nyaomaru), a frontend engineer just back from a short vacation on Texel, a small island in the Netherlands. 😸🏝️

Today, let's talk about a type guard that looks completely safe.

```ts
const isUser = (value: unknown): value is User => {
  // runtime checks...
};
```

Looks good, right?

TypeScript knows that when `isUser(value)` returns `true`, the value is a `User`.

But there's a small problem

> TypeScript trusts that promise.

It doesn't prove that your runtime checks actually validate every field in `User`.

And that's where type guards can slowly drift away from the types they claim to protect.

Let's take a look! 👀

![Image description](https://dev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com/uploads/articles/q57ad2z2634ibsugdrxh.png)

---

## 🕳️ A Type Guard Can Become Outdated Without an Error

Imagine we start with this type

```ts
type User = {
  id: string;
  name: string;
};
```

And a hand-written type guard

```ts
const isUser = (value: unknown): value is User => {
  if (typeof value !== "object" || value === null) {
    return false;
  }

  const candidate = value as Record<string, unknown>;

  return typeof candidate.id === "string" && typeof candidate.name === "string";
};
```

So far, everything matches.

Later, we update `User`

```ts
type User = {
  id: string;
  name: string;
  role: "admin" | "member";
};
```

But we forget to update the guard.

```ts
const isUser = (value: unknown): value is User => {
  if (typeof value !== "object" || value === null) {
    return false;
  }

  const candidate = value as Record<string, unknown>;

  return typeof candidate.id === "string" && typeof candidate.name === "string";
};
```

There is no `role` check.

But this still compiles. 😿

---

## 🧠 Why Doesn't TypeScript Catch This?

Because this

```ts
(value: unknown): value is User
```

is a user-defined type predicate.

You are telling TypeScript

> Trust me. If this function returns `true`, the value is a `User`.

TypeScript can check whether the declared predicate type itself makes sense.

But it cannot generally prove that arbitrary runtime logic actually validates every part of that type.

So this is possible

```ts
const isUser = (_value: unknown): _value is User => true;
```

Terrible guard.

Perfectly valid TypeScript. 😹

The return type is a contract written by us, not a proof generated from the function body.

---

## 🔄 This Becomes a Maintenance Problem

The annoying part isn't writing the guard once.

It's keeping these two things synchronized over time

```text
TypeScript type
      ↕
Runtime validation
```

Types change.

Properties get

- added
- removed
- renamed
- made optional
- changed to another type

And every time that happens, we need to remember that some runtime guard somewhere may also need an update.

If we forget, the compiler may not tell us.

That's the kind of bug I really don't want to rely on memory to prevent.

---

## ✅ What If the Type Could Be the Contract?

This is one of the reasons I added `typedStruct` to `is-kit`.

Suppose the application type already exists:

```ts
type User = {
  id: string;
  name: string;
  age?: number;
};
```

We can build the guard against that existing type

```ts
import { isNumber, isString, optionalKey, typedStruct } from "is-kit";

const isUser = typedStruct<User>()({
  id: isString,
  name: isString,
  age: optionalKey(isNumber),
});
```

Now the field map has a type-level relationship with `User`.

At runtime, it still performs ordinary object validation.

But at compile time, TypeScript can check whether the guards we declared match the object type they're supposed to follow.

---

## 💥 Now Drift Becomes Visible

Let's add a field again

```ts
type User = {
  id: string;
  name: string;
  role: "admin" | "member";
  age?: number;
};
```

But forget to update the guard

```ts
typedStruct<User>()({
  id: isString,
  name: isString,
  age: optionalKey(isNumber),

  // TypeScript error:
  // role is missing
});
```

Nice.

The runtime bug became a compile-time problem.

The same thing happens if the guard uses an incompatible field type

```ts
import {
  isNumber,
  isString,
  oneOfValues,
  optionalKey,
  typedStruct,
} from "is-kit";

typedStruct<User>()({
  id: isString,

  name: isNumber,
  // TypeScript error:
  // User["name"] is string

  role: oneOfValues("admin", "member"),
  age: optionalKey(isNumber),
});
```

This is the part I care about most.

`typedStruct` doesn't eliminate maintenance.

It makes forgotten maintenance visible.

---

## 🧩 Optional and Nullable Are Different

Another place where object guards can get confusing is optional properties.

Consider:

```ts
type User = {
  id: string;
  nickname?: string | null;
};
```

There are two separate ideas here

```text
nickname may be absent
```

and

```text
nickname may exist with the value null
```

Those are different runtime contracts.

With `typedStruct`

```ts
import { isString, nullable, optionalKey, typedStruct } from "is-kit";

const isUser = typedStruct<User>()({
  id: isString,
  nickname: optionalKey(nullable(isString)),
});
```

Now

```ts
isUser({ id: "user-1" });
// true

isUser({
  id: "user-1",
  nickname: null,
});
// true

isUser({
  id: "user-1",
  nickname: "Neko",
});
// true

isUser({
  id: "user-1",
  nickname: 42,
});
// false
```

I like keeping these two decisions explicit:

- `optionalKey(...)` → the property may be absent
- `nullable(...)` → the value may be `null`

They look similar at first, but they describe different things.

---

## 🌳 Nested Types Don't Need to Be Duplicated Either

Now imagine a larger type:

```ts
type Account = {
  readonly id: string;

  readonly profile: {
    readonly displayName: string;
    readonly bio: string | null;
  } | null;

  readonly tags: readonly string[];
};
```

We could manually copy the profile shape into another type.

But that creates another thing that can drift.

Instead, we can reference the type we already have

```ts
import { arrayOf, isString, nullable, typedStruct } from "is-kit";

const isProfile = typedStruct<NonNullable<Account["profile"]>>()({
  displayName: isString,
  bio: nullable(isString),
});

const isAccount = typedStruct<Account>()({
  id: isString,
  profile: nullable(isProfile),
  tags: arrayOf(isString),
});
```

This is the model I like:

> Reuse the existing type at compile time. Compose small guards at runtime.

The application type remains the source we want the guard to follow.

---

## 🔒 What About Extra Runtime Properties?

There is another distinction worth making.

These are two different questions:

1. Does my **guard definition** match the TypeScript type?
2. Should a **runtime object** be allowed to contain additional properties?

By default, an object can still have additional keys.

If you want the runtime object shape to be closed as well, you can enable exact mode:

```ts
import { isString, typedStruct } from "is-kit";

type User = {
  id: string;
  name: string;
};

const isExactUser = typedStruct<User>()(
  {
    id: isString,
    name: isString,
  },
  {
    exact: true,
  },
);
```

Then:

```ts
isExactUser({
  id: "user-1",
  name: "Ada",
});
// true

isExactUser({
  id: "user-1",
  name: "Ada",
  debug: true,
});
// false
```

Whether extra properties should be rejected is a runtime policy decision.

It shouldn't be confused with keeping the guard definition synchronized with the TypeScript type.

---

## ⚖️ Which Should Be the Source of Truth?

I don't think there is one correct validation style for every project.

The important question is

> What already owns the shape of this data?

### Manual predicate

```ts
const isSomething = (value: unknown): value is Something => {
  // custom logic
};
```

Great when the validation is unusual or not primarily structural.

### Guard-first

```ts
const isUser = struct({
  id: isString,
  name: isString,
});
```

Useful when the guard itself should define the resulting type.

### Type-first

```ts
const isUser = typedStruct<User>()({
  id: isString,
  name: isString,
});
```

Useful when `User` already exists and the runtime guard needs to stay aligned with it.

### Schema-first

A schema library or code generation may be the better source of truth when you need things like:

- structured validation errors
- coercion
- transforms
- defaults
- generated artifacts

These solve different problems.

> I don't think every boolean validation check needs to become a schema. 😸

---

## 🚫 What `typedStruct` Does Not Do

There are some important boundaries.

`typedStruct` does **not** generate runtime validation from a TypeScript type.

Types are erased at runtime, so you still need to declare the guards you want to execute.

It also doesn't:

- prove that every custom predicate is honest
- coerce values
- return rich structured validation errors
- replace schema-first workflows
- validate numeric or symbol properties as part of its string-keyed object contract

It's intentionally smaller than that.

The goal is simply to create a typed bridge between

> the object type you already have

and

> the runtime guards you choose to run

---

## 🎯 The Important Part

The main point isn't really `typedStruct`.

It's this

> **A type predicate is a promise, not a proof.**

This

```ts
(value): value is User
```

doesn't mean TypeScript inspected your implementation and proved that every `User` field was validated.

We made that promise.

So when a TypeScript type is the source of truth, I think it's useful to make the runtime guard structurally depend on that type instead of relying on us to remember every future change.

That's what I wanted `typedStruct` to help with. 😸

If your guard defines the type, use a guard-first approach.

If an existing TypeScript type should define the contract, connect the guard to that type.

And if you need rich parsing, transforms, coercion, or detailed errors, that's where a schema starts to earn its weight.

I wrote a more complete guide about this on the is-kit documentation site:

{% embed https://is-kit.dev/guides/keep-type-guards-in-sync %}

If you like small reusable TypeScript type guards, `is-kit` is open source too!

{% embed https://github.com/nyaomaru/is-kit %}

Thanks for reading! 🙌

Congrats to the DEV Weekend Challenge: Dog Days Edition Winners!

The results are in for our [DEV Weekend Challenge: Dog Days Edition](https://dev.to/devteam/join-our-dev-weekend-challenge-dog-days-edition-1000-in-prizes-across-five-winners-submissions-1g4i)! Thank you for your patience while we worked through judging, and thank you for the volume and quality of what you sent us.

We asked you to build something for, about, or inspired by dogs ahead of International Dog Day. What came back was even cuter than expected: from an OS for dogs to several canine translators and a wide array of doggie dashboards. We had so much fun going through your submissions.

Now, let's celebrate our winners!

